      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the address-limiter fixture (50 attacking addresses at 100/s each, a limiter keyed on address admitting 20/s), at least 90% of attack requests are refused, at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (address limiter), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Negative twin: a variant of the base whose limiter limit variable starts at 1,000/s per address refuses no attack request and the claim must fail. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "address-limiter-false-positives-bounded",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the address-limiter fixture, legitimate users grouped onto addresses (up to 50 behind one shared address) see a false-positive refusal ratio of at most 0.001, at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (address limiter), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Negative twin: a variant of the base whose limiter limit variable starts at 2/s per address refuses users behind shared addresses and the claim must fail. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "bulkhead-keeps-static-served-under-db-slowdown",
      "kind": "measured",
      "statement": "On the hold-across fixture (16 API threads held while calling the database, a database slowdown of 10 times from 20 s to 40 s, static requests at 200/s), with database calls limited to 8 threads and a maximum wait, the p99 of static requests in the slowdown window is at most 50 ms, at 95% confidence over 59 runs.",
      "rationale": "Fixture specified in docs/simulation.md, Fixtures (holding across a downstream wait), with base, control and twins; planned (WG-4715). Parameters are tagged assumed or to be measured; a holding result is model-holds, never supported. Control: no slowdown. Negative twin: collapse-classes on the thread pool lets database calls hold all 16 threads, static requests starve, and the claim must fail; in the control it must not. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code",
        "seeded-streams-independent"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "metric-claims-calibrated-or-unvalidated",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A metric claim reads unvalidated when it names no calibration, when a calibration fails on its hold-out load runs (distinct from the fitting runs, with equal workload, capacities and window; the sign-test interval of the model median over at least 6 kept runs, with no truncated run, outside the measurement band widened over repeats), when its evidence has expired at the asOf date recorded in the result or is bound to another component revision, or when a calibration tolerance exceeds the margin computed from the beta-quantile of the per-run statistic; it is flagged extrapolated outside the convex hull of the tested points with the same fault set, decided in exact rational arithmetic; and its result lists first the assumed and to-be-measured parameters, and pairs of flagged parameters, whose variation over their declared ranges flips the verdict.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Calibration and sensitivity). Negative twin: a calibration whose hold-out run is also its fitting run must be refused; a hold-out run whose measured p99 is twice the model's must fail and leave the claim unvalidated; a calibration with 10% tolerance on a claim with a 5% margin must not validate it; a fixture whose verdict flips when the page-read class limit is doubled must list that parameter. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "metric-claims",
        "performance-metrics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "crash-instance-loses-state",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A crash-instance fault stops exactly the named instance at its time: its incarnation is incremented, its timers removed, its waiting requests removed with their timeouts, its in-service requests ended with their service-done entries and its units released, its restart drawn, its notify event broadcast; every targeted entry created for the old incarnation, a reply to a call it made before the crash included, is dropped as stale; a routed entry decided for it is ignored by that behavior only, and a broadcast skips it; instance aggregates exclude it while it is down; round-robin, random, any and least-busy routes skip it; a restart edge restores only its own incarnation; a crash of a missing or already down instance does nothing; and on restart the instance is in its initial state with its initial variables.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715, defined once here for WG-4704's crash-restart; specified in docs/simulation.md, Faults in format 2). Negative twin: client C calls server S, C crashes and restarts, S replies: the reply must be dropped as stale; two overlapping crashes of one instance must not restart it at the first restart edge; engine mutants that deliver the reply to the new incarnation, restart early, or route to a down instance because it holds nothing fail it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "simulation-engine",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "distribution-lowering-bit-exact",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "simulationInput lowers distributions with integer arithmetic only: the exponential and normal quantile tables are produced by the integer algorithms of docs/simulation.md and equal the correctly rounded values at the 1,045 grid points, their canonical text hashing to SHA-256 a23cd2a9566c106701375d5cf75ef982b8db6f3e52c199c47e6239649c840950; exp_fp, ln_fp and the exponential, log-normal and empirical constructions reproduce every reference output bit for bit; and a lowered value of 2^53 base units or more, a sigma above 8 or a p99 above 10^6 times the median is refused.",
      "rationale": "Built (WG-4715) in src/core/distributions.js. Negative twin: an implementation that evaluates the log-normal quantile with Math.exp or AS 241 in doubles, rounds sigma instead of flooring it, or computes the normal CDF at 2^-96 instead of 2^-192, must fail the table hash or a reference output; a log-normal of median 1 ms and sigma 9 must be refused. The correctly rounded values were checked once against an 80-digit computation when the algorithms were specified; the test pins them through the hash.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "simulation-contract"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "poisson-arrivals-by-inversion",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Poisson arrivals, constant or ramped, take exactly one arrival draw per instant, computed by inverting the integrated rate with exact integer square roots and rounding to the nearest base unit; no valid input is refused or fails for rejected candidates, and a draw whose instant falls at or after its phase end or the horizon creates nothing.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Workloads). Negative twin: a ramp from 0 to 10^7/s over 1000 s in unit ns, which would need about 1.4 x 10^5 rejections under thinning, must run with one draw per instant; an implementation that rounds interarrivals down instead of to the nearest unit must fail the fixture that measures the arrival rate of a constant phase at a mean interarrival of 100 base units. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "workload-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "overlapping-faults-follow-active-set",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "With windowed faults that overlap on one resource, the value in effect at every fault-edge is the declared function of the active set: the last active set-capacity, set-class-limit or replace-demand in the order of the input's faults list, the product of the active scale-demand factors reduced to lowest terms, and collapsed while any collapse-classes is active; demand and scale are evaluated at the grant against the faults active then; when a collapse ends, waiting requests return to their own classes in (since, seq) order; and under a collapse each request keeps its class for maxWait and metrics.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Faults in format 2). Negative twin: two overlapping set-capacity faults whose order in the input is swapped must give different capacities during the overlap; an engine mutant that applies the most recently started fault instead of the last in input order, or that leaves requests in the collapsed queue after the collapse ends, fails it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "resource-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "broadcasts-evaluated-at-delivery",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A broadcast reaches the instances that are up when it is delivered (keyed instances created since its creation and instances restarted since included) and skips down ones without an ignored entry; targeted entries keep the instance and incarnation fixed when they were created; routed entries are decided, with their counters, draws and incarnation, when first taken as head, and a delayed copy keeps the decision; and the next arrival instant of a workload is created exactly once, even when a fault delays or drops the first entry of the current instant.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4664, WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Instances and Workloads). Negative twin: a broadcast at time 10 to a behavior whose instance crashed at 5 and restarted at 8 must reach the restarted instance while a reply created at 7 for its old incarnation is dropped; an arrival created at 6 and routed least-busy, delivered at 9, must see the load at 9 and must not be stale; a delayed first arrival must not create the next instant twice. Engine mutants that route at creation or recreate the instant fail it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "simulation-engine",
        "workload-model",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "request-lifecycle-defines-outcomes",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Every request of a class follows the request lifecycle of docs/simulation.md: it starts when its start entry is first taken as head; it ends once, at the first delivered end event with its key (a deliver step that does not fail, whether or not a transition fires), as error at once when its start entry is dropped, crashed, stale or handled by no transition, or as timeout on a closed-client timeout; it is censored otherwise; only ok requests are finite samples; and every sample count, bucket power and derived metric is computed from these outcomes.",
      "rationale": "Planned (WG-4715; specified in docs/simulation.md, Request lifecycle). Negative twin: a fixture whose start event is dropped by a drop-event fault, whose start event reaches a behavior with no transition for it, and whose start is routed to a crashed instance must record three error ends at the step that lost them, and a p99 claim over it must count them as +infinity; a metrics implementation that leaves lost starts out of the samples, or ends a request on a dropped end event, fails it. Not evaluated until recorded evidence exists.",
      "subjects": [
        "performance-metrics",
        "metric-claims",
        "formal-semantics"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "temporal-claims-checked-on-all-runs",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A never, precedes or eventually claim reported by exegra prove as holding has a Lean theorem, accepted by Lean, that the verified explorer found the form true on every complete run of its scenario (every order of simultaneous events, every subset of its faults, each run ending at quiescence, at the step limit or in a failed step); a reported counterexample is a real run that violates it, and a path the bound or the state budget cuts off is reported incomplete.",
      "rationale": "WG-4698. The forms are defined in formal/Exegra/Temporal.lean with the meaning checkTrace gives them; tcheck_sound and tcheck_counterexample_real are proved with only Lean's standard axioms, pinned with #guard_msgs. The test runs exegra prove on tests/fixtures/race: an eventually that holds, an eventually that fails because a path drops the event and ends first, a precedes that holds, and a precedes that the scripted run satisfies and a reordering violates. Each result is discharged by native_decide, so it also trusts the Lean compiler. Exegra records the test as evidence; it does not check the proofs itself.",
      "subjects": [
        "exhaustive-explorer",
        "proof-export"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "metric-claims-in-specifications",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A specification that declares resources, workloads, request classes and a format-2 scenario derives a version-2 engine input (times in base units, rates exact, distributions lowered), and a metric-bound claim over it evaluates to model-holds, violated or inconclusive with its run count and confidence, never verified or supported.",
      "rationale": "WG-4729, docs/simulation.md, Specification-level form and Claims over metrics. Negative: a bound the model misses must read violated with a replayable seed.",
      "subjects": [
        "metric-claims",
        "workload-model",
        "resource-model"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "runtime-data-copies-fresh-and-related",
      "kind": "structural",
      "statement": "Every copy of Exegra's runtime data that this specification declares (session model and claim results, scene, simulation run, browser preferences and camera memory, command-line outputs, generated documents, bundles, proof outputs and the production bundle's embedded specifications) states a freshness bound, its holder is related through declared relationships to the owner of every record it copies, and no shared copy holds principal data.",
      "rationale": "Checked by the data-model method over all records and copies. erasure-point and party-by-id are not listed: Exegra stores no personal data, so they would hold only vacuously.",
      "subjects": [
        "data-model",
        "specification-text",
        "interaction-state",
        "recorded-results",
        "session-claim-results",
        "session-preferences",
        "shipped-specifications"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "relationships-match-imports"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "data-model",
        "rules": [
          "freshness-bound",
          "holder-reaches-owner",
          "no-principal-in-shared"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "session-starts-after-engine",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "At start-up the session is started only after the engine has loaded or failed to load.",
      "subjects": [
        "visual-interaction",
        "simulation-engine",
        "session",
        "app-start-and-open"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "precedes",
        "scenarios": [
          "app-start-and-open"
        ],
        "first": {
          "event": "engine-load-settled"
        },
        "then": {
          "event": "session-init-requested"
        },
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "app-start-and-open",
            "faults": [
              "session-started-before-engine"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "open-takes-disk-hashes",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "After start-up the opened project is saved and its base hash is the hash of the file on disk, so the first write is checked against what was read.",
      "subjects": [
        "session",
        "persistence-client",
        "workspace-service",
        "read-project",
        "app-start-and-open"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "eventually",
        "scenarios": [
          "app-start-and-open"
        ],
        "expr": "project-session-flow.projectName == 'exegra' && project-session-flow.baseHash == workspace-disk-flow.diskSpec && project-session-flow.saveState == 'saved'",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "app-start-and-open",
            "faults": [
              "open-ignores-read-hashes"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "read-only-build-never-writes",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "A production build never asks to write a project file, even after an edit.",
      "subjects": [
        "session",
        "build-system",
        "production-read-only-edit"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "never",
        "scenarios": [
          "production-read-only-edit"
        ],
        "occurs": {
          "event": "write-intent"
        },
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "production-read-only-edit",
            "faults": [
              "readonly-build-writes"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "reload-adopts-disk-text",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "After a conflict resolved by reloading, the session holds the text on disk, reports saved, and the other process's change was never overwritten.",
      "subjects": [
        "session",
        "workspace-service",
        "conflict-resolved-by-reload"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "eventually",
        "scenarios": [
          "conflict-resolved-by-reload"
        ],
        "expr": "project-session-flow.textHash == workspace-disk-flow.diskSpec && project-session-flow.saveState == 'saved' && workspace-disk-flow.specWrites == 0",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "conflict-resolved-by-reload",
            "faults": [
              "session-reload-keeps-text"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "overwrite-writes-session-text",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "After a conflict resolved by overwriting, the file on disk holds the session's text and the session reports saved.",
      "subjects": [
        "session",
        "workspace-service",
        "write-file",
        "conflict-resolved-by-overwrite"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "eventually",
        "scenarios": [
          "conflict-resolved-by-overwrite"
        ],
        "expr": "workspace-disk-flow.diskSpec == project-session-flow.textHash && project-session-flow.saveState == 'saved'",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "conflict-resolved-by-overwrite",
            "faults": [
              "session-overwrite-keeps-stale-base"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "external-change-is-adopted",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "A change made on disk by another process while nothing is pending is adopted: the session ends holding the text on disk with that text as its base hash.",
      "subjects": [
        "session",
        "workspace-service",
        "file-change-notice",
        "external-change-adopted"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "eventually",
        "scenarios": [
          "external-change-adopted"
        ],
        "expr": "project-session-flow.textHash == workspace-disk-flow.diskSpec && project-session-flow.baseHash == workspace-disk-flow.diskSpec",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "external-change-adopted",
            "faults": [
              "change-notice-lost"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation",
      "rationale": "The negative is a lost notice, a failure of the environment, not a defect: it shows that adoption rests on the change stream, which does not re-send a change made while it was down."
    },
    {
      "id": "adopted-change-never-written-back",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "Text adopted from disk is never written back by the session.",
      "subjects": [
        "session",
        "external-change-adopted"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "never",
        "scenarios": [
          "external-change-adopted"
        ],
        "occurs": {
          "event": "write-intent"
        },
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "external-change-adopted",
            "faults": [
              "session-writes-back-external-change"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "own-write-never-reloaded",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "The notice of the session's own write never makes it reload the project.",
      "subjects": [
        "session",
        "workspace-service",
        "own-write-notice-ignored"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "never",
        "scenarios": [
          "own-write-notice-ignored"
        ],
        "occurs": {
          "event": "reload-requested"
        },
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "own-write-notice-ignored",
            "faults": [
              "session-reloads-own-write"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "undo-saved-to-disk",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "After an edit and its undo, the model holds the original text, the file on disk holds it too and the session reports saved.",
      "subjects": [
        "transactions",
        "session",
        "workspace-service",
        "undo-restores-and-saves"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "eventually",
        "scenarios": [
          "undo-restores-and-saves"
        ],
        "expr": "project-history-flow.specHash == 'h1' && workspace-disk-flow.diskSpec == project-history-flow.specHash && project-session-flow.saveState == 'saved'",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "undo-restores-and-saves",
            "faults": [
              "session-skips-flush-on-undo"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "undo-restores-earlier-text",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Whenever a step can be redone, the model holds the text from before the undone edit.",
      "subjects": [
        "transactions",
        "undo-restores-and-saves"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "state-invariant",
        "scenarios": [
          "undo-restores-and-saves"
        ],
        "expr": "project-history-flow.redoDepth == 1 => project-history-flow.specHash == 'h1'",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "undo-restores-and-saves",
            "faults": [
              "undo-keeps-text"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation"
    },
    {
      "id": "accepted-writes-journaled",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "In these runs every accepted write of spec.jsonc or layout.jsonc has its journal line.",
      "subjects": [
        "workspace-service",
        "write-journal",
        "undo-restores-and-saves",
        "layout-move-saved"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "state-invariant",
        "scenarios": [
          "undo-restores-and-saves",
          "layout-move-saved"
        ],
        "expr": "workspace-disk-flow.journalLines == workspace-disk-flow.specWrites + workspace-disk-flow.layoutWrites",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "undo-restores-and-saves",
            "faults": [
              "journal-append-fails"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "faultModel": "local-workstation",
      "rationale": "The negative is a failure of the environment: the journal is appended after the file is replaced, so a failed append leaves an accepted write without a line. The journal is not written for imports, recorded results, generated files or changes made outside Exegra at all (record write-journal)."
    },
    {
      "id": "layout-move-writes-layout-only",
      "kind": "temporal",
      "statement": "A dragged node is saved to layout.jsonc and spec.jsonc is not written.",
      "subjects": [
        "transactions",
        "session",
        "layout-file",
        "layout-move-saved"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "machines-abstract-the-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "eventually",
        "scenarios": [
          "layout-move-saved"
        ],
        "expr": "workspace-disk-flow.diskLayout == 'L2' && workspace-disk-flow.specWrites == 0 && project-session-flow.saveState == 'saved'",
        "negatives": [
          {
            "scenario": "layout-move-saved",
            "faults": [
              "move-edits-spec"
            ]
          }
